messages:
alex: quick heads-up — running into two separate issues when packaging the Node/TS SDK for edge runtimes (Vercel/Next edge and Vite SSR edge). 1) ESM/CJS entry confusion: deployed edge function fails with a runtime parse error: `Unexpected token 'export'` referencing files in node_modules/@redwood/inference-sdk/dist. Locally in dev mode everything is fine. 2) streaming auth weirdness: when proxying a streaming response from the edge to our hosted API, the Authorization header sometimes stops being present mid-stream (client disconnects, or the upstream sees an empty auth). repro + snippets below.

alex: minimal repro (Next edge / package.json):
```
// package.json
{
  "name": "edge-play",
  "type": "module",
  "dependencies": {
    "@redwood/inference-sdk": "^1.4.0"
  }
}

// app/route.js (edge)
export const config = { runtime: 'edge' };
export default async function handler(req) {
  const res = await fetch('https://api.redwood.example/v1/generate', {
    method: 'POST',
    headers: { Authorization: req.headers.get('authorization') },
    body: req.body
  });
  return new Response(res.body, { headers: { 'content-type': 'text/event-stream' } });
}
```

mira: a couple questions: which bundler(s) are you hitting this on — Next only or also Vite SSR? Is the parse error at cold start or after some SaaS optimization step? :thinking:

alex: primarily Vercel Edge functions (Next with runtime: 'edge') and a separate Vite SSR edge target. Error occurs immediately on first invocation. stacktrace indicates the bundler pulled `dist/index.cjs` but runtime treated it as ESM so the `export` syntax crashed.

jon: likely bundler is resolving the wrong entrypoint. Common fixes:
- force the ESM entry via rollup/webpack/vite alias or using resolve.mainFields
- add a tiny ESM shim in the app that re-exports the bits you use so bundler keeps the ESM module

example shim:
```js
// src/redwood-client.mjs
import sdk from '@redwood/inference-sdk';
export const createClient = sdk.createClient;
export default sdk;
```
Then import from `./src/redwood-client.mjs` in your edge code.

samir: on the streaming/auth: some edge platforms strip or rewrite headers when you return a Response that directly streams another fetch's body. The safest approach is to explicitly read `req.headers.get('authorization')` and attach it to the fetch upstream (you already do), and then stream the response manually so headers/forwarding behavior is controlled. Example pipe:
```js
export default async function handler(req) {
  const auth = req.headers.get('authorization') || '';
  const upstream = await fetch('https://api.redwood.example/v1/stream', { headers: { Authorization: auth } });
  const reader = upstream.body.getReader();
  return new Response(new ReadableStream({
    async pull(controller) {
      const { done, value } = await reader.read();
      if (done) { controller.close(); return; }
      controller.enqueue(value);
    }
  }), { headers: { 'content-type': 'text/event-stream', 'cache-control': 'no-transform' } });
}
```
This avoids letting the platform implicitly copy headers which can cause partial or missing values mid-stream.

lane: also verify bundler externals. Vite/Next sometimes tries to bundle a CJS file into the edge bundle which breaks. For Vite SSR add:
```ts
// vite.config.ts
export default defineConfig({
  ssr: { noExternal: ['@redwood/inference-sdk'] }
});
```
For Next try toggling `esmExternals`:
```js
// next.config.js
module.exports = { experimental: { esmExternals: 'loose' } };
```
Both tend to force the module resolver to prefer the ESM path instead of inlining the CJS build.

mira: +1. Also from a packaging POV, ensure the SDK's package.json has a precise `exports` map, something like:
```
"exports": {
  ".": {
    "import": "./dist/index.mjs",
    "require": "./dist/index.cjs"
  }
}
```
That helps bundlers pick the correct entry. If `module`/`main` are ambiguous some toolchains fallback unpredictably.

alex: good notes. short-term I'll add jon's shim and document it in the repo. longer-term we'll update the SDK `exports` and add a note for Next/Vite configs. for streaming, samir's manual piping seems most robust. i'll open a PR with the shim + an example edge handler and link the repro.

ci-bot: CI green for branch `edge-proxy-fix` (4 tests) :white_check_mark:

jon: ping @eng-platform on the PR and include the minimal repro zip, happy to review.

alex: will do. thanks y'all :heart:
